{
  "image_name": "520bfcc4b998c15f483aa7b8c19ab159.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts how rainfall moves through a pre-urban landscape versus an urbanized one, highlighting how urban development reshapes the water cycle. On the pre-urban side, precipitation falls onto vegetated, permeable ground, where much of the water infiltrates into the soil and feeds groundwater and steady baseflow. Trees also return water to the atmosphere through evaporation and transpiration, and surface runoff appears limited.\n\nOn the urban side, houses, infrastructure, and less permeable surfaces reduce infiltration and lower both evaporation and transpiration. As a result, more rain becomes rapid, often polluted runoff that is routed downslope and into drainage pathways, increasing erosion. The diagram also shows reduced groundwater recharge and reduced baseflow overall, although leaks from water and sewer pipes and over-irrigation may partly add to baseflow in developed areas. In karst settings, the figure suggests that urban drainage can create preferential flow paths.\n\nOverall, the image shows a shift from a slower, vegetation- and groundwater-dominated hydrologic system to a flashier urban system with less infiltration, less natural water recycling, more runoff, and altered streamflow behavior.",
